{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>I am HaShem your Elohim, etc.</b> The reason for the doubling of <b>who brought you out</b> <b>from the land</b> etc. <b>from the house of slaves</b> — the plain sense of the verses is that He intends to magnify the enormity of their captivity, that they were in the land of Egypt, a place whose prisoners He did not open (Isaiah 14:17) etc., in addition to the exceeding pollution of that place, and further that they were there in the category of slaves — and HaShem brought us out." "<b>Further,</b> HaShem may intend to say it in this manner: <b>I am HaShem your Elohim</b> — behold, at the beginning of HaShem's words He mentioned two matters: the first, that He is HaShem blessed be He, the Name of Havayah — this declares the faith that from Him blessed be He and by His utterance was everything that comes into being, and all that was and all that will be; it also declares His existence blessed be He — He was, He is, and He will be. And the second, He said <b>your Elohim</b> — meaning that they are obligated to accept His utterance and His decrees, and whoever rebels against His mouth, Eloah will strike him. And corresponding to these two aspects, the divine names come to establish each one with a proof against which there is no argument. Correspond to His saying <b>I am HaShem</b> — for you this is the sign: <b>who brought you out from the land of Egypt.</b> Know that through the actual Exodus from Egypt, all those who speak falsehood were denied — heaven forbid that there is any faith other than His — for the luminaries were denied, for they are not elohim, when HaShem darkened their light; the seas were denied, for HaShem performed signs upon them and turned them to blood; the earth was denied when HaShem struck it and it swarmed with evil swarms; living creatures were denied, for there is no power in them or in their constellations; all divinities were denied when HaShem executed judgments upon them. Behold, that HaShem is the Master of all existence and rules over existence and can annihilate it and decrees upon nothingness and it comes to be, as the eyes of all Israel saw. And corresponding to what He said <b>your Elohim</b> — that they must accept Him upon themselves as an elohah to fulfill all His words — He stated the reason for the matter: <b>from the house of slaves</b>, meaning, since you were a slave to another and I brought you out from their slavery, the line of justice dictates that you accept My divinity upon yourself to fulfill every word." "<b>Further,</b> it intends in the doubling of <b>from the land of Egypt, from the house of slaves</b> to make known a hidden sod, as is known to those who know grace (חן), that the souls of Israel who came out of Egypt — these are they that were sunk in the impurity of Egypt, and through our father Jacob and his sons who went down to Egypt, they brought them out, as He said (Genesis 46:3) ״כִּי לְגוֹי גָּדוֹל אֲשִׂימְךָ שָׁם״. And see what I explained there. And those who know hidden things said to HaShem — for it is He Himself of whom the verse says (Deuteronomy 4:7) ״כִּי מִי גוֹי גָּדוֹל״ etc. — who stood at Mount Sinai. And accordingly the verse is properly explained: His saying <b>who brought you out from the land of Egypt</b> is the bringing out of the souls from their captivity in the impurity of Egypt — meaning He refined them through His counsels blessed be He — but they were still in Egypt in the house of slavery. And He said <b>from the house of slaves</b> corresponding to the fact that He brought out the refined people so that their enemies would not enslave themselves through them." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend according to the saying of the Sages of blessed memory (Jerusalemite Sukkah 5:4) in explanation of ״יוֹשֵׁב תְּהִלּוֹת יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — that the Holy One blessed be He was not satisfied with the praise of those who praise Him when they said ״Elohim of the universe״ and not ״Elohim of the angels״, but only when they said ״בָּרוּךְ ה׳ אֱלֹהֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״. And this is His saying <b>I am HaShem your Elohim</b> — meaning, I was not satisfied with any epithet of exaltation except the appellation <b>your Elohim</b>, and through this He publicized the greatness of Israel among the supernal beings. And corresponding to the proclamation among the lower beings He said <b>who brought you out</b> etc. — meaning, a place from which there is no possibility of departure, and your exodus was a great proclamation for honor and for glory. Go out and learn from the words of Jethro, who was one singled out from the nations. And His saying <b>from the house of slaves</b> — meaning, I did not bring you out in a manner that would leave upon you the name of servitude. The parable: if HaShem had brought them out by way of flight and the like, even though the deliverance would have been certain, nevertheless the name of servitude would not have been uprooted from them, for they are still ״servants of Pharaoh.״ But from the fact that He brought them out with several awesome wonders, until Pharaoh said ״You are your own, you are free men״ etc. (Yalkut, R''H), and further when he pursued and HaShem performed the great wonder in drowning him in the sea — through this the name of servitude was voided from them." "<b>Further,</b> He intends to speak with the soul and with the body. Corresponding to the soul, which recognizes the aspect of spirituality, He said <b>I am HaShem your Elohim</b>, for the soul recognizes the aspect of divinity. And perhaps at that very instant the soul departed and recognized its Eloah when He said to it ״I am״ (אנכי), and therefore He spoke in the singular <b>your Elohim</b>, for the aspect of holy souls is unified, and there is no separation in them in the world of separation, which is this world. And you will find that David said in the words of the Chronicles (I Chronicles 17:21): ״וּמִי כְעַמְּךָ יִשְׂרָאֵל גּוֹי אֶחָד בָּאָרֶץ״ — meaning, even on earth, in the place of separation, they are one nation, for they became sanctified and were made on earth like angels; and this is the praise with which David peace be upon him praised them. And corresponding to the aspect of the body and its vitality, which is the aspect that resembles animal vitality, He declared the recognition of eternal lovingkindness — for you cannot grasp with the desire of illuminated spirituality." "<b>Further</b>, it intends: <b>I am HaShem your Elohim</b> — meaning, before your coming into this world, for you are a light from the holy light, the portion of HaShem, as it says (Deuteronomy 32:9): ״חֵלֶק ה׳ עַמּוֹ״. And this aspect the word ״אֱלֹהֶיךָ״ declares, for this matter can be attributed only to the aspect of holiness that is from His light, blessed be He, as is known to those who know grace (חֵן), and as hinted in many sayings of our Sages of blessed memory. And His saying <b>who</b> etc. — meaning, who on this account brought you out of the land of Egypt; and by this He gave the reason why HaShem afflicted and shook the world and its inhabitants, the heavens and their hosts, for Israel's sake, to bring them out — for it is inconceivable that He would not rescue the portions of holiness and draw them near to Him." "<b>Further</b>, it intends along this line: <b>I am HaShem</b> — meaning, at the time when I deal graciously with you, I will be your Master; and at the time when I discipline you and reprove you, I will be your Elohim." "<b>Further</b>, it intends in the manner of the verse (Psalms 116:4): ״כּוֹס יְשׁוּעוֹת״ etc. ״וּבְשֵׁם ה׳ אֶקְרָא״ ״צָרָה וְיָגוֹן״ etc. ״וּבְשֵׁם״ etc., and our Sages of blessed memory expounded (Berakhot 60a) that one blesses over the bad with joy just as [over the good], and so forth; and the reason is that the aspect of discipline from HaShem is only for the sake of good and kindness. And this is His saying <b>I am HaShem</b> — meaning, the attribute of kindness and mercy — <b>even at the time of</b> your Elohim — meaning, when I discipline you." "<b>Further</b>, it intends in His saying <b>from the land of Egypt, from the house of slaves</b>, along this line: I am the one who brought you out of the land of Egypt; I am the one who will yet bring you out of the house of slaves. And this alludes to the final exile, in which every nation and tongue enslaved Israel in the manner customary for slaves, and HaShem hinted that He will bring us out of this house." "<b>Further</b>, it intends along this line: <b>who brought you out of the land of Egypt</b>. And if you will say: why did You bring us out from there and not increase Your kindness with us, that HaShem would establish Israel there in the land of their enemies, so that they would enslave their laborers and rule over them and take their land from their hand and dwell in it before their eyes — and this would bring greater satisfaction of spirit to Israel and a greater declaration of the might of omnipotence, that there is Elohim who judges and rules in the earth — therefore HaShem said that the reason for the matter is because that land is a house of slaves, meaning, in the manner of the verse (Deuteronomy 32:8): ״בְּהַנְחֵל עֶלְיוֹן״ etc. ״יַצֵּב גְּבֻלֹת עַמִּים״, and our Sages of blessed memory said (Zohar 1:108b) that HaShem apportioned the places of the world to the heavenly princes, except for the land of Canaan, which He chose for His name, as it were. And this is His saying <b>from the house of slaves</b> — meaning, a place that belongs to the slaves of HaShem, meaning that its governance is the governance of one prince from among the servants of HaShem; and HaShem did not want Israel to be under the governance of the princes but under His own governance, blessed be He, in all things. And He brought them out of the land of Egypt because it is a house of slaves, in order to give them a land that is the house of Elohim."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:22:46.889927729Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}